== Introduction
<introduction-8>
This special weekend session covers practical rulings of #strong[Eid
al-Aḍḥā] and #strong[Qurbānī (Uḍḥiyah)] based on the short kitāb
_Aḥkām e Eid al-Aḍḥā wa Qurbānī_ by the late #strong[Muftī Muḥammad
Shafīʿ ʿUthmānī] (raḥimahullāh), former Grand Muftī of Pakistan. The
goal is to revise and refresh these masāʾil before the blessed days
begin.

#horizontalrule

== Faḍāʾil: Virtues of the First Ten Days of Dhul Ḥijjah
<fadail-ten-days>

The first ten days of Dhul Ḥijjah are the #strong[best days of the
entire Islamic calendar] (after Ramaḍān). Allah has taken an oath in
their name:

#quote(block: true)[
وَالْفَجْرِ وَلَيَالٍ عَشْرٍ

#emph["By the dawn, and by the ten nights."]

#strong[Ref:] Sūrah al-Fajr (89:1--2)
]

Whenever Allah takes an oath by something, it indicates its immense
value.

#strong[Key virtues (from aḥādīth as cited in class):]
- One fast during these ten days equals the reward of #strong[one year]
  of fasting.
- The ʿibādah of one night among these ten nights equals the reward of
  #strong[Laylat al-Qadr].
- The #strong[9th of Dhul Ḥijjah] (Yawm al-ʿArafah) is the most
  special: fasting on it expiates sins of #strong[one year before and
  one year after].

#horizontalrule

=== Recommended: Not Cutting Nails and Hair
<not-cutting-nails-hair>

A person who intends to slaughter an animal should #strong[not cut their
nails or hair] during these ten days --- resembling one in the state of
iḥrām. This is a recommended practice (mustaḥabb), not obligatory.

- Trim nails and settle hair #strong[before] the ten days begin.
- If nails or hair become unsightly or cause difficulty (e.g., moustache
  interfering with eating), they #strong[may be cut] --- removing harm
  takes priority over gaining the benefit of a recommended act.
- For those sending Qurbānī abroad: wait until the slaughter is done
  there before cutting, to achieve this sunnah fully.

#horizontalrule

== Takbīr al-Tashrīq
<takbir-tashreeq>

It is #strong[wājib] to recite the Takbīr al-Tashrīq after every
#strong[farḍ ṣalāh] from the #strong[Fajr of the 9th of Dhul Ḥijjah]
to the #strong[ʿAṣr of the 13th of Dhul Ḥijjah] --- spanning five days
and twenty-three prayers.

- Obligatory on #strong[both men and women], whether praying with
  jamāʿah or alone.
- #strong[Men] recite it audibly (not excessively loud --- just
  audible).
- #strong[Women] recite it quietly.
- It is recited #strong[once] after each farḍ prayer.

#horizontalrule

== Sunan of Eid Day
<sunan-eid-day>

The following acts are mustaḥabb / masnūn on the day of Eid al-Aḍḥā:

+ Waking up early in the morning.
+ Taking a bath (ghusl).
+ Using miswāk.
+ Wearing the #strong[best available] clothing --- not necessarily
  expensive; just the best one has.
+ Applying perfume (ʿiṭr).
+ #strong[Not eating before the Eid prayer] --- the first thing to eat
  should be the uḍḥiyah meat (the meat from one's sacrifice). This is
  the "hospitality from Allah's side." Recommended, not obligatory.
+ Reciting takbīr (#emph[Allāhu Akbar]) while walking to the muṣallā
  --- in a moderate voice.

#horizontalrule

== Eid Prayer --- Key Rulings
<eid-prayer-rulings>

The Eid prayer is #strong[wājib]. It has a specific methodology with
#strong[six additional takbīrāt]:

- #strong[First rakʿah:] Three extra takbīrāt #strong[before] the
  qirāʾah.
- #strong[Second rakʿah:] Three extra takbīrāt #strong[after] the
  qirāʾah.
- In each takbīr, raise the hands and let them drop --- #strong[except]
  the third takbīr of the first rakʿah, because qirāʾah follows it
  (hands remain folded).

#strong[Easy rule:] 3 before (first rakʿah), 3 after (second rakʿah).
Raise and drop each time, except the one before qirāʾah.

#strong[If the Imām misses the takbīrāt:] No sajdah sahw is performed.
Reason: large gathering --- sajdah sahw could create confusion and
fitnah among people who lack detailed knowledge.

#strong[Khuṭbah after Eid prayer:] It is masnūn to listen. One should
not leave or get engaged in other activities.

#horizontalrule

== Qurbānī (Uḍḥiyah) --- Status and Virtues
<qurbani-status>

Qurbānī is one of the greatest acts of ʿibādah during these days. It is
a remembrance of the #strong[Sunnah of Ibrāhīm] (ʿalayhi al-salām),
who was commanded to sacrifice his son in a dream and proceeded to obey.

#strong[Virtues (from aḥādīth as cited in class):]
- In exchange of every #strong[hair] of the animal, there is one reward.
  If the animal has wool, every strand carries a reward.
- The person is #strong[forgiven] before the first drop of blood falls
  on the ground --- provided the intention is sincere.

